[{"id":"VRCE-5-31-1","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":31,"verse":1,"english":"Thereupon coming down from the tree, Hanumān, the highly effulgent son of Wind, having a coral-red countenance and attired in a humble guise, approached Sītā and joining reverentially his palms, addressed her with the following sweet words.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-33-1"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5031001","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"2167efb41dac87c4c21174d204061a53c51563a3c7049b89b372ca0b5333e45d","compact":"41f309fdb459977773aae0825a7461941f8c2441d32563f1141dfa57c36debfd","nasal":"e7ea0ad82db494b0fa52d1b21ca8e9b5b0121088411500dffffa6c2fab1cb21a"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-31-2","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":31,"verse":2,"english":"Who are you, O you having eyes like lotus petals and wearing a soiled silken cloth, who are waiting there holding a branch of the tree? Why are the tears of sorrow flowing from your eyes like to drops of water falling from lotus petals?","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.716,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-33-2"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5031002","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"562d533698d04a8a49034bba4633ce9fc5afce814b238f88abc3dfe8b32e917c","compact":"a3d9e91f68133b7823d38d0cd91b551abaa0fdc3af36f01b2c17f469c78e138a","nasal":"5fcc01e71d4b7cb0d32c98bff9e20b139d9c37dbe11d18ad4bcd55940694804f"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-31-3","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":31,"verse":3,"english":"Who are you, O you having eyes like lotus petals and wearing a soiled silken cloth, who are waiting there holding a branch of the tree? Why are the tears of sorrow flowing from your eyes like to drops of water falling from lotus petals?","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-33-2"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5031003","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"34e04e70b2a4ea88f27c35fa7ca81ba4a90a12b132b539b3730e2c9582260cd5","compact":"8480af250a2e713e3c28cabcbe6af34dc2bb1f310dcbbdabdfe8fe27c743019e","nasal":"34dca94177a931529be4834e53358b1b4bf51524e2c7e03659b20df7dd15280b"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-31-4","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":31,"verse":4,"english":"Who are you, O most fair of face, among the celestials, Asuras, Nāgas, Gandharvas, Rāks asas, and Yaksas and Kinnaras? Do the Rudra's (The Rudras are manifestations of Śiva) claim your birth O you fair damsel,? Or the swift gods who ride the storm? Or the Vasus? (A kind of demi-gods of whom six are enumerated viz., Dhava, Druva. Soma or the Moon, Visnu, Anila or wind, Anala or fire, Prabhusa and Prabhava) You appear to me as of a celestial race.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-33-3"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5031004","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"7831275396d1347c4c970719084a4e5be035a73e3fd4093d3f02f2f8bc74fc61","compact":"e8cfaa8a7cfd6766209bce139ea8c5cf5d486f2a748c1270fdb704c0e7d09f7f","nasal":"e72d620f4386adf31ad6d290b7ccf625f4b830e3fd97f237a035a2b4cff5747e"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-31-5","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":31,"verse":5,"english":"Who are you, O most fair of face, among the celestials, Asuras, Nāgas, Gandharvas, Rāks asas, and Yaksas and Kinnaras? Do the Rudra's (The Rudras are manifestations of Śiva) claim your birth O you fair damsel,? Or the swift gods who ride the storm? Or the Vasus? (A kind of demi-gods of whom six are enumerated viz., Dhava, Druva. Soma or the Moon, Visnu, Anila or wind, Anala or fire, Prabhusa and Prabhava) You appear to me as of a celestial race.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-33-3"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5031005","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"01f300ea07168f5c08e5d7e188eb6987a73688367c91288ddd495bc22f73bf06","compact":"da357dbe8db2b82e6a1a71c887713ba1287bd8d98a51fb51817ea1c2715e1793","nasal":"da357dbe8db2b82e6a1a71c887713ba1287bd8d98a51fb51817ea1c2715e1793"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-31-6","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":31,"verse":6,"english":"Are you Rohiņā, the best and most accomplished of the stars, who, left from the Moon, has fallen down from the abode of the celestials?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.917,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-33-4"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5031006","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"3ebd5572cdbffc9808fc46243bcf2e723623bd67c8d09bba39ec2d68bab25671","compact":"e8599bb205b9dbc8cad189275cbac74af9bd95750a11bbb0cdb44d4a552fb33e","nasal":"41548514d931e528261d869620087ff95feff412473e79a418981f7a6dbae99c"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-31-7","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":31,"verse":7,"english":"Who are you O blessed dame, O you having blameless eyes? Are you, O lotus-eyed damsel, the blessed Arundhuti, who has fled in wrath or jealous pride form the side of her lord (Vasistha)?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.761,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-33-5"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5031007","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"c4ad73cde5bceb3a905b30c39f13ac6f57f5ee73d39d74b81e0471d4ce188ab7","compact":"7390b561e3f5e1c3f30d01b6be19895056b2d6c88a4c206bb9c72ce790a6a54f","nasal":"ee5d67afee76e5d27ecda9faa46c17c488bc0e487054c93d54e4950e41456a37"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-31-8","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":31,"verse":8,"english":"Or O lovely damsel, who is the son, father, brother or husband gone from this world for whom you are weeping?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.823,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-33-6"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5031008","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"6a472a0a3019c097907e73e455ace4c5ccffba6ed011c220992842a4f9bb2102","compact":"e3a2f9dd748392702aa13785dc110b6c28108c2a860f5e590d670f46939a154d","nasal":"e3a2f9dd748392702aa13785dc110b6c28108c2a860f5e590d670f46939a154d"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-31-9","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":31,"verse":9,"english":"Yet by your tears and sighs, by the earth you are treading and by calling on a monarch's name it appears that you are not a celestial. (The celestials never shed tears nor touch the ground when they walk.) But from the marks on your person it appear that you are either the consort or the daughter of a king.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.875,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-33-7"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5031009","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"714764645c13eb4fe78ae1f055b0655232a4e4dce32b13a4a6a94cde427e3bb2","compact":"55a873ac2d3a4d29cc285047643862d03f7deb2d6b2d945bfc8e0e7754433768","nasal":"eb16c7874b52314be0476fc08199db2c5733f4edd06136f67876e8b48f609ff1"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-31-10","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":31,"verse":10,"english":"Are you that Sītā, I do ask you, who had been stolen and borne away by Rāvana from Janasthāna? May good betide you!","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.807,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-33-8"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5031010","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"ef2e0020b594aa44a20d4a00af99faff513e71600393bf9b2b3a351370953150","compact":"b38091d48cdd3137479b343b190c99d93854592cdd0a356e9bf89700bce1acf1","nasal":"b38091d48cdd3137479b343b190c99d93854592cdd0a356e9bf89700bce1acf1"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-31-11","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":31,"verse":11,"english":"Hearing those words of Hanumān and greatly delighted at Rāma's name, Vaidehī spoke to him.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.859,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-33-10"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5031011","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"9a412806ca0c6ac237761bda538e1795eb4ab3ae7c6b9dcc3d9794174a54f3d8","compact":"88726a46ac84672c54e4637776aa2887fb3ac8c34028cda9bc842b90613593bd","nasal":"4dc471460e738dce108a6356aec62370fa72e7963f49f74d10018858115a6a7b"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-31-12","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":31,"verse":12,"english":"I am the daughter-in-law of Dasaratha, knowing his own self, the foremost of the kings of the world and the slayer of the enemy's host, and the daughter of Janaka the high-souled king of Vaideha. My name is Sītā and I am the consort of the highly intelligent Rāma.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.702,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-33-11"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5031012","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"a2f2216cf00123316fc63b8682a5cf706242ed4ffcc4d82ad3419e571f401ea5","compact":"1f0a88e5cb2758ece79eb36ad09abdfb4a4966f937efd2534da04ffd9c2d0bf7","nasal":"1f0a88e5cb2758ece79eb36ad09abdfb4a4966f937efd2534da04ffd9c2d0bf7"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-31-13","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":31,"verse":13,"english":"I lived twelve years in Rāghava's abode, enjoying every earthly comfort and satisfying every desire.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-33-12"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5031013","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"e8fcbfb686e25e1074e70b5e8434233fc1c6232160f0318f26747fcbc8bf6b35","compact":"07c399688155279c8109472d9bfed1d1784bf881fb5f14389fa591ce232ba3da","nasal":"c46b93f8caca36c8dc346f2d809be2b2b4b49c52c1f11c8a96a6802fda88a625"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-31-14","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":31,"verse":14,"english":"And at the beginning of the thirteenth year the king, advised by his priest, arranged for the installation of the descendant of Ikşvākus (Rāma) on the throne.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.881,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-33-13"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5031014","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"278338cc173852b43689aed7dd3f2549f313a628badf5e404888b994f7eef0a7","compact":"f76cfaf73ad67c48f1d2af29274eeb5dfaf2e40ecedadddf7dd0c0fb35edfd7d","nasal":"eaa1e523cf94eff0fadf2af6b91d0c21add8571d0d5baf107bbb1cc174a00e0f"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-31-15","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":31,"verse":15,"english":"And while the articles for the installation were being collected, the queen Kaikeyi addressed her lord saying.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.644,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-33-14"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5031015","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"c78fd8169ba05279bc942f9da81d7e0db8d3eb831e7d4e62d818dc6e70faba22","compact":"c9a8b5e242f79a9e575a3f12cc83f4866ab3dfaa8960ced3e700a81d093e7e80","nasal":"c9a8b5e242f79a9e575a3f12cc83f4866ab3dfaa8960ced3e700a81d093e7e80"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-31-16","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":31,"verse":16,"english":"I shall not drink or eat for days and this shall be the end of my existence if Räma be installed.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-33-15"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5031016","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"4c1236f0e8e463e7cb931368ba0727d9544f6ccd69028dbb239f3f89d2743364","compact":"3a6009e8a2173583b71e7d09b0243e4976b734ea3c0f75b24f6287c4f2dd8b57","nasal":"6eb522ff9138d76dabdce504fe1954b54d5b9369ee1a642182d93a9dddee76ea"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-31-17","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":31,"verse":17,"english":"If you do not wish to falsify the vow (This refers to the vow which Dasaratha made to Kaikeyſ at the time of the war going on between the celestials and the Asuras) you made to me,O best of kings, let Rāma then repair to the forest.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-33-16"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5031017","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"2a1961c034d580f6e250c95aef2eadff2f0addf4fb9fa268f1d8fc493fd622f3","compact":"dfd4f12b8a955ff0d40405498e24add24831913f991ba4110ac477fab5f6a633","nasal":"656896feaa0dcb995c36383d9e3976e8d68502bc426d66fbc3eb7c91621962cd"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-31-18","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":31,"verse":18,"english":"The monarch was truthful and remembering his vow and hearing the unpleasant and vile words of Kaikeyi lost himself (in grief).","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-33-17"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5031018","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"250b0f77a06a126c6a3dd8ff8fc6cb96b46b0f0543f31f2041d22b56568f2318","compact":"33fe026b9765b315e26f9eebdf2dc1b71ee0a37aff8d53d7d39c2db00d2626cc","nasal":"33fe026b9765b315e26f9eebdf2dc1b71ee0a37aff8d53d7d39c2db00d2626cc"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-31-19","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":31,"verse":19,"english":"Thereupon the old king abiding by truth, weeping, begged of his eldest son, the kingdom.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.909,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-33-18"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5031019","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"971020ca020ef8f7139283da813323b572fbba432a9650659793527097443df3","compact":"f0bc72432089c395a61b82e69d1c15000017d1ded4ed1444e33789274a87c0a8","nasal":"f0bc72432089c395a61b82e69d1c15000017d1ded4ed1444e33789274a87c0a8"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-31-20","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":31,"verse":20,"english":"The graceful Räma killed his sire's command more than the installation, and resolving within him, promised to obey his words.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-33-19"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5031020","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"5d71d414309df6d7f80392331648a664c1e7e817255ff4c5d97b6ddaaff1e23a","compact":"b88f5445ebebbfe8c571c328d667039e9f13ad3fad86edf5414324fbdfc7c705","nasal":"4676d6cd04c0b82c43a978d9c5a05b38e815caf5901be97a9611e908141b6d80"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-31-21","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":31,"verse":21,"english":"Rāma, having truth for his prowess, never, for his life, takes back what her gives, never speaks an untruth and always speaks truth.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.464,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-33-20"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5031021","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"39a3035f904bc6b6b307d3a24058ae25b8a6e2bbad3dc106ef6309f3a667b1b1","compact":"3319b2dfddd025b0362495dd76981336a93f3bda5162dd51cec4d16e29956496","nasal":"388cd90ebd93bfc91205428b69d6f8d9757c02752c306aa307e78c0bad42dc04"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-31-22","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":31,"verse":22,"english":"Leaving aside his costly attire, the highly glorious (Rāma) renounced with all his heart the kingdom and gave me over to his mother.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-33-21"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5031022","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"636e10a633c3a19ebdf78bdd9e2f0625b5764d5986b26232c9e0764a1a18aba5","compact":"4937f9d0875df8bbc0fb654df549fb1d37744937873feaf39901896eb4d0af09","nasal":"4937f9d0875df8bbc0fb654df549fb1d37744937873feaf39901896eb4d0af09"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-31-23","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":31,"verse":23,"english":"But myself wearing the garb of an ascetic, repaired speedily before him. Separated from him, I do not like to live even in the celestial region.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-33-22"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5031023","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"3c018fccbdcdbbcd34f7328fa55424f88967ce82fcbcd2262bdb3f1245cacc7d","compact":"ff0e47f3c28b2b4901427f9651bc67f9076bb8b0e99696bf20e46fb193ffc505","nasal":"ff0e47f3c28b2b4901427f9651bc67f9076bb8b0e99696bf20e46fb193ffc505"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-31-24","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":31,"verse":24,"english":"The great son of Sumitrā, the enhancer of friends' joys, to follow his elder brother, dressed himself in bark and kusa.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.691,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-33-23"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5031024","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"3bfa48ee9e6aa963ed26ca654915cfa7db7b6af7af474dcc0ef6f3d24ac95bc6","compact":"29f97dd918bb1d46ab3244394c2b0c34393463c29c4e1d4a208623f196c2c2dd","nasal":"07cfa4f7e00d5b2e62613ba28a94aaca947368e2d84ccdf2310971326b9f5200"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-31-25","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":31,"verse":25,"english":"Thus abiding by our sire's behest, and taking firm vows we entered a dreary forest never seen before.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.881,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-33-24"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5031025","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"c345ec6796bc16837ca7d6cbee1b4b0b2d6814bd9a9ae4e0485bc633c210a8a1","compact":"febd4f28523ed8f0ea0314eb954d777835fb1417647872914eb0bb83dfe36e9f","nasal":"fdc077bc4dea7d92cf554ff4b8bdef8fc172d54c1c66719e2d7e8b9f356ef658"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-31-26","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":31,"verse":26,"english":"While that one, of unmitigated effulgence was thus living in the forest of Dandaka, I, his consort, was stolen by the vicious-souled Rākşasa—Rāvaņa.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-33-25"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5031026","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"5f09b8eab47e38e716ecc02142892f9e9613f89cbd16efc0906c902c391bc0a8","compact":"e6a91459b03a24f8b817f2439a870ef2fdfac6d8cf6e8ecf1979183cbc02e65d","nasal":"cca9337bc942640692f22083ab46b7098c5bccba57d60d6190a5a67dedffef29"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-31-27","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":31,"verse":27,"english":"He has kindly allowed me two months respite after which I shall be slain.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-33-26"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5031027","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"b681e34957a72681f2d4451dd699576e2e725010336017aad0cfb8e9aa88ba5c","compact":"d7d309c